{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves, numbered I to XII from front to back. Some are sensory, some motor and some mixed. Apart from the olfactory and optic nerves, they attach to the brainstem, except the accessory nerve, which arises from the upper cervical spinal cord. They supply the eye, face, neck, pharynx, larynx and tongue.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN62.1" phase="Phase 1" /%}

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/cranial-nerves/cranial-nerves-brainstem-base-overview.webp" alt="Diagram of the underside of the brain with the twelve cranial nerves labelled I to XII where they leave the brainstem." caption="The cranial nerves seen from below, numbered from front to back." credit="OpenStax, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1320_The_Cranial_Nerves.jpg" width="784" height="491" /%}

## What are the cranial nerves?

The cranial nerves are the 12 pairs of nerves that leave the cranial cavity. Each one is named and numbered with a Roman numeral in order from front to back (rostrocaudal order).

- Some are **purely sensory**, some are **purely motor**, and the rest are **mixed**, carrying both sensory and motor fibres.
- Some also carry **parasympathetic fibres** (the part of the autonomic system that stimulates glands and the smooth muscle inside the eye). These fibres are secretomotor, meaning they make glands secrete.
- Branches of the oculomotor, trochlear, trigeminal, abducens, facial, glossopharyngeal, vagus, accessory and hypoglossal nerves supply muscles of the eyeball, face, neck, pharynx, larynx and tongue.
- The trigeminal, glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves carry general sensation from the skin of the face, part of the scalp, the lining of the mouth, nose, pharynx and larynx, the cornea, the meninges and the skull.
- The olfactory, optic, trigeminal, facial, vestibulocochlear and vagus nerves carry the special senses: smell, vision, hearing, balance and taste.

The **olfactory nerve is the only sensory cranial nerve that goes straight to the cerebral cortex** rather than relaying through the thalamus. The optic nerve ends in the thalamus. The other ten pairs attach to the brainstem, except the accessory nerve, which attaches to the upper cervical spinal cord.

A cranial nerve can be affected by a disease of the nerve itself, by a disease of the brain or meninges, or secondary to a systemic disorder.

## The twelve nerves at a glance

