## Functional components and nuclei

In the brainstem, the grey matter forms separate columns. The motor columns are **medial** and the sensory columns are **lateral**. The head needs seven columns in all, three motor and four sensory. This is because the two extra columns for the branchial apparatus (the structures of the embryonic head and neck that form the pharynx and larynx) and one extra column for the special senses are added to the four columns of the spinal cord. Each column breaks into smaller pieces, and these form the **nuclei** (clusters of nerve cell bodies) of the cranial nerves.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/cranial-nerves/brainstem-cranial-nerve-nuclei.webp" alt="Colour-coded diagram of the brainstem showing the cranial nerve nuclei, with motor nuclei placed medially and sensory nuclei laterally." caption="Cranial nerve nuclei in the brainstem, with motor columns medial and sensory columns lateral." credit="Nadezdha D. Kiriyak, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Representative_illustration_of_the_brainstem_demonstrating_the_normal_configuration_of_the_cranial_nerves_and_their_associated_nuclei._The_vagus_nerve_originates_from_the_nucleus_ambiguus_of_the_medulla.webp" width="894" height="1277" /%}

Locations of the main nuclei:

- **Oculomotor nucleus:** midbrain, at the level of the superior colliculus
- **Trochlear nucleus:** midbrain, at the level of the inferior colliculus
- **Trigeminal:** motor nucleus in the upper pons; mesencephalic nucleus in the midbrain (proprioception); superior sensory nucleus in the upper pons (touch and pressure); spinal nucleus from the upper pons down to the C2 segment of the spinal cord (pain and temperature of the face)
- **Abducent nucleus:** lower pons
- **Facial:** motor nucleus, nucleus of the tractus solitarius (taste), superior salivatory nucleus and lacrimatory nucleus, all in the lower pons
- **Vestibulocochlear:** two cochlear nuclei and four vestibular nuclei, at the junction of the medulla and pons
- **Glossopharyngeal:** nucleus ambiguus, inferior salivatory nucleus (for the parotid) and the nucleus of the tractus solitarius (taste from the posterior third of the tongue), all in the medulla
- **Hypoglossal nucleus:** runs almost the whole length of the medulla, just beneath the floor of the fourth ventricle, near the midline

## I. Olfactory nerve

The olfactory cells are **bipolar neurons** (16 to 20 million in man) that lie in the olfactory part of the nasal mucosa. They are both the receptors and the first neurons of the smell pathway.

- Their central processes form the olfactory nerves, about 20 in number. They pass through the **cribriform plate** of the ethmoid and synapse in glomeruli with cells of the olfactory bulb.
- The **mitral and tufted cells** of the bulb give off fibres that form the olfactory tract. It reaches the primary olfactory cortex (anterior perforated substance, periamygdaloid and prepiriform areas).
- Fibres from there go on to the secondary olfactory cortex (the entorhinal area, in the uncus and anterior parahippocampal gyrus) and to the orbitofrontal cortex.
- The pathway bypasses the thalamus. Many of its target areas belong to the limbic system, which is why smells can affect emotion and behaviour.

**Testing:** each nostril is tested separately, with the eyes closed, using a mild smell such as toothpaste or coffee. Food flavours depend on smell, not on taste.

**Clinical points:** loss of smell is called **anosmia**. It can follow a fracture of the anterior cranial fossa or a frontal lobe tumour, and it can follow a head injury, especially a fall on the back of the head. The brain shifts and shears the fine olfactory filaments where they pass through the cribriform plate. After head injury, loss of aromatic flavour is often mistaken for loss of taste, because the basic tastes are unimpaired. Anosmia on one side only is not usually noticed by the patient. Allergic rhinitis causes temporary loss of smell.

## II. Optic nerve

The optic nerve carries vision. Most fibres of the optic tract end in the lateral geniculate body. A few go to the pretectal nucleus and the superior colliculus and are concerned with light reflexes. The optic radiation then runs to the visual cortex.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/cranial-nerves/visual-pathway-diagram.webp" alt="Diagram of the visual pathway from both eyes through the optic chiasm and tract to the lateral geniculate body and visual cortex." caption="The visual pathway from the retina to the visual cortex." credit="Rachel Sanchez Thwing, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visual_Pathway_to_the_Brain.jpg" width="960" height="720" /%}

**Testing:** ask about changes in eyesight, test visual acuity with a chart (a Snellen chart at 6 metres), test the visual fields by confrontation (comparing the patient's field with your own), and look at the retina and optic disc with an ophthalmoscope.

**Papilloedema:** the subarachnoid space extends forward around the optic nerve to the back of the eyeball. The retinal vein crosses this space. A rise in cerebrospinal fluid pressure squeezes the thin vein wall, causing congestion of the retinal veins, oedema of the retina and a bulging optic disc.

**Trauma:** a fracture of the sphenoid bone can lacerate the optic nerve and cause blindness from the start. The pupil does not react to light shone in that eye but reacts to light shone in the opposite eye (the consensual reflex).

## III, IV and VI: the nerves of eye movement

Each eye is moved by six muscles: four rectus (superior, inferior, medial, lateral) and two oblique (superior and inferior). Three nerves control them: III, IV and VI. Their nuclei talk to each other through the **medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)**, so that the two eyes move together. Brainstem gaze centres, driven by the cortical eye fields, coordinate horizontal and vertical gaze.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/cranial-nerves/extraocular-muscles-nerve-supply.webp" alt="Diagram of the eyeball and orbit showing the six extraocular muscles and the cranial nerves that supply them." caption="The extraocular muscles and the nerves (III, IV and VI) that move the eye." credit="File:Eye orbit anterior.jpg: Patrick J. Lynch, medical illustrator derivative work: Shyhed, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 2.5" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eye_orbit_anterior_(modified).jpg" width="960" height="640" /%}

**Oculomotor nerve (III)**

- It emerges from the anterior surface of the midbrain, passes between the posterior cerebral and superior cerebellar arteries, and runs in the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus. It divides into a superior and an inferior ramus, which enter the orbit through the superior orbital fissure.
- The somatic motor fibres supply levator palpebrae superioris, superior rectus, medial rectus, inferior rectus and inferior oblique.
- The parasympathetic fibres supply the constrictor pupillae and the ciliary muscle. They synapse in the ciliary ganglion and reach the eye in the short ciliary nerves.
- It is entirely motor.

**Trochlear nerve (IV)**

- It is the slenderest cranial nerve. It crosses to the opposite side and leaves the **posterior** surface of the midbrain. It supplies the superior oblique.
- Damage causes double vision (diplopia) on looking down, which is corrected by tilting the head away from the side of the affected eye. It often appears alone after a minor head injury.

**Abducens nerve (VI)**

- Its nucleus is in the lower pons. It supplies the lateral rectus and turns the eye laterally.

## V. Trigeminal nerve

The trigeminal nerve has three divisions.

- **Ophthalmic (sensory):** cornea, skin of the forehead, scalp, eyelids and nose, and the lining of the paranasal sinuses and nasal cavity
- **Maxillary (sensory):** skin over the maxilla and upper lip, teeth of the upper jaw, lining of the nose, maxillary sinus and palate
- **Mandibular (motor and sensory):** motor to the muscles of mastication, mylohyoid, anterior belly of digastric, tensor veli palatini and tensor tympani; sensory to the skin of the cheek, lower lip and side of the head, the teeth of the lower jaw, the temporomandibular joint, and the lining of the mouth and anterior two-thirds of the tongue

**Testing:**

- Sensation (touch, pain, temperature) over all three divisions: forehead, cheek and jaw line
- Motor: clench the teeth and feel temporalis and masseter, open the mouth against resistance, move the jaw side to side (pterygoids), and look for jaw deviation
- **Corneal reflex:** afferent limb is CN V, efferent limb is CN VII
- **Jaw jerk:** both limbs are CN V, centre in the mid-pons

**Trigeminal neuralgia (tic douloureux)** is the most common neuralgia (a neuralgia is pain along the course of a nerve).

- It is classically **one-sided** and usually in the V2 or V3 area.
- The pain is like an electric shock, lasts seconds to minutes, and comes in paroxysms with pain-free gaps. It is often set off by a small **trigger zone** on the cheek, lip or nose, touched by chewing, facial movement or contact.
- It affects middle-aged and elderly people, and is more common in women. There is no objective loss of skin sensation.
- Common causes are compression of the nerve by the superior cerebellar artery where it leaves the brainstem, a cerebellopontine angle tumour, and multiple sclerosis.
- **Carbamazepine** is the drug of choice, with a risk of agranulocytosis (a dangerous fall in white cells). If drugs fail, **microvascular decompression** of the nerve is the most widely used operation.

## VII. Facial nerve

The facial nerve has two parts that usually merge inside the internal acoustic meatus: a **motor root** to the muscles of the face, and the **nervus intermedius**, carrying taste fibres and parasympathetic secretomotor fibres to glands. It leaves the pons at the pontomedullary junction and enters the internal acoustic meatus with the vestibulocochlear nerve.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/cranial-nerves/facial-nerve-course-temporal-bone.webp" alt="Diagram of the facial nerve from the brainstem through the temporal bone to the parotid gland, with its segments and branches labelled." caption="The course and branches of the facial nerve from the brainstem to the face." credit="Patrick J. Lynch, medical illustrator, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 2.5" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_facial_nerve_branches_TZBMC.jpg" width="960" height="805" /%}

**Course in three parts:**

{% table %}
- **Part**
- **Segment and length**
- **Key points**
---
- **Intracranial**
- Pons to internal acoustic meatus, 15 to 17 mm
- Passes across the cerebellopontine angle
---
- **Intratemporal**
- Meatal segment, 8 to 10 mm
- Inside the internal acoustic meatus
---
- **Intratemporal**
- Labyrinthine segment, 4.0 mm
- Fundus of the meatus to the geniculate ganglion, where the nerve turns (the first **genu**). The shortest and narrowest segment
---
- **Intratemporal**
- Tympanic (horizontal) segment, 11.0 mm
- Geniculate ganglion to just above the pyramidal eminence; above the oval window, below the lateral semicircular canal
---
- **Intratemporal**
- Mastoid (vertical) segment, 13.0 mm
- Pyramid to stylomastoid foramen. The second genu lies between the tympanic and mastoid segments
---
- **Extracranial**
- Stylomastoid foramen to the peripheral branches
- Crosses the styloid process and passes through the parotid gland
{% /table %}

The **labyrinthine segment** is the narrowest part of the nerve and lies in the narrowest part of the bony canal. It also lacks anastomosing arterial cascades. So oedema or inflammation can easily compress the nerve and cause paralysis.

**Branches in the temporal bone:**

- **Greater petrosal nerve:** arises at the geniculate ganglion and carries secretomotor fibres to the lacrimal gland and to the glands of the nasal mucosa and palate
- **Nerve to stapedius:** arises at the level of the second genu

**Terminal branches** in the face: temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular and cervical.

**Testing:** ask the patient to raise the eyebrows (frontalis), shut the eyes tight (orbicularis oculi), show the teeth, smile and blow the cheeks (buccinator), close the lips (orbicularis oris), and pull down the corners of the mouth (platysma). Also look for facial asymmetry: loss of forehead wrinkling, drooping of the corner of the mouth, a flattened nasolabial fold and a wide palpebral fissure. Taste on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, lacrimation and hyperacusis (abnormal sensitivity to sound) are also checked.

## VIII. Vestibulocochlear nerve

This nerve carries hearing (cochlear part) and balance (vestibular part). It is tested with a tuning fork, finger rub and caloric testing.

**Rinne and Weber tests** use a 256 or 512 Hz tuning fork in a quiet room. The two tests help tell two kinds of hearing loss apart:

- **Conductive hearing loss (CHL):** sound cannot get through the outer or middle ear. Causes include otosclerosis, otitis media, wax (cerumen) and otitis externa.
- **Sensorineural hearing loss (SNHL):** the inner ear or the nerve is not working.

{% table %}
- **Test**
- **How it is done**
- **Normal result**
---
- **Rinne**
- A vibrating fork is placed on the mastoid, then beside the ear, and the patient says which is louder
- Sound beside the ear (air conduction, AC) is louder than on the mastoid (bone conduction, BC), because the middle ear bones amplify sound
---
- **Weber**
- A vibrating fork is placed in the midline on the vertex of the skull
- The sound is heard equally in both ears
{% /table %}

{% table %}
- **Finding**
- **Conductive loss**
- **Sensorineural loss**
---
- **Rinne**
- BC louder than AC (Rinne negative)
- AC still louder than BC (Rinne positive or normal), but both are diminished
---
- **Weber**
- Lateralises to the **affected** ear
- Lateralises to the **normal** ear
{% /table %}

The main use of these tests is not general screening. It is to make a first separation of SNHL from CHL in a patient with unilateral hearing loss or tinnitus.

**Caloric test:** cool or warm water is run into one ear canal. Cold water gives a fast component of nystagmus to the opposite side, and warm water gives it to the same side (**COWS**: cold opposite, warm same).

**Two conditions to know:**

- **Meniere's syndrome:** recurrent attacks of tinnitus, vertigo and hearing loss, with sensitivity to noise, in middle-aged or older people. The volume of endolymph is increased.
- **Acoustic neuroma:** a slow-growing benign tumour of neurolemmal cells that causes early hearing loss.

## IX. Glossopharyngeal nerve

The glossopharyngeal nerve is the nerve of the third branchial arch.

- **Motor:** stylopharyngeus
- **Secretomotor:** parotid gland (from the inferior salivatory nucleus)
- **Sensory:** pharynx, tonsil, soft palate, tympanic cavity, posterior third of the tongue, carotid body and carotid sinus
- **Taste:** posterior third of the tongue

It leaves the skull through the jugular foramen, in front of the vagus and accessory nerves and in its own dural sheath. It then passes between the internal jugular vein and the internal carotid artery and descends in front of the artery. The **carotid branch** carries impulses from chemoreceptors in the carotid body and baroreceptors in the carotid sinus wall.

**Clinical points:**

- Damage to this nerve alone is rare. It is usually seen along with other lower cranial nerves.
- **Glossopharyngeal neuralgia** gives paroxysms of burning or stabbing pain in the tonsil, posterior pharynx, back of the tongue and middle ear. It is rare, about 5% as common as trigeminal neuralgia, and can be set off by swallowing, talking or touching the tonsils.
- The gag reflex has CN IX as its afferent limb and CN X as its efferent limb.

## X. Vagus nerve

The vagus is a large mixed nerve with a longer course and wider distribution than any other cranial nerve. It runs through the neck, thorax and abdomen.

It leaves the skull through the jugular foramen behind the glossopharyngeal nerve, in a common sheath with the accessory nerve. In the neck it descends in the **carotid sheath**, between the internal jugular vein and the internal carotid artery. The right vagus crosses the first part of the subclavian artery to enter the thorax. The left vagus enters the thorax between the left common carotid and subclavian arteries.

{% table %}
- **Branch**
- **Supply**
---
- **Meningeal**
- Dura of the posterior cranial fossa
---
- **Auricular**
- Pinna, external acoustic meatus and tympanic membrane (sensory)
---
- **Pharyngeal**
- Joins the pharyngeal plexus. Carries fibres from the cranial root of the accessory nerve. Motor to all muscles of the pharynx except stylopharyngeus and all muscles of the soft palate except tensor veli palatini
---
- **Carotid body branches**
- Carry impulses from baroreceptors and chemoreceptors
---
- **Superior laryngeal**
- Internal laryngeal nerve: sensory to the larynx down to the vocal cords and to the piriform fossa. External laryngeal nerve: motor to cricothyroid, close to the superior thyroid artery
---
- **Recurrent laryngeal**
- Motor to all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except cricothyroid; sensory to the mucosa below the vocal cords
---
- **Cardiac branches**
- Go to the deep cardiac plexus
{% /table %}

**Why "recurrent"?** The right recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around the first part of the subclavian artery. The left hooks around the arch of the aorta. Both then ascend in the groove between the trachea and oesophagus, closely related to the inferior thyroid artery.

## XI. Accessory nerve

The accessory nerve has two roots.

- The **spinal root** arises from the upper cervical spinal cord (C1 to C5), ascends alongside the cord and enters the skull through the foramen magnum.
- It turns laterally to meet the **cranial root**. The two share the jugular foramen, then separate.
- The **cranial root** joins the vagus and is distributed in its branches to the soft palate, pharynx (through the pharyngeal plexus) and larynx. Usually the cranial root is counted as part of the vagus, and only the **spinal root is called CN XI**.
- The spinal root enters the deep surface of **sternocleidomastoid**, supplies it, then crosses the posterior triangle of the neck to supply **trapezius**.

**Injury:** the nerve lies fairly superficially in the posterior triangle, so it can be injured at operation or by a penetrating wound. Trapezius is paralysed and wastes, and the **shoulder drops**. The patient has difficulty raising the arm above the head after abducting it to a right angle with deltoid. If the nerve is sacrificed in a radical neck dissection, the unsupported weight of the shoulder and arm pulls on the brachial plexus and can cause intractable neuralgia.

**Testing:** ask the patient to turn the head to one side against resistance, which tests the sternocleidomastoid of the **opposite** side. Then ask the patient to shrug the shoulders, which tests trapezius.

## XII. Hypoglossal nerve

The hypoglossal nerve is **purely motor**. It supplies all the muscles of the tongue except palatoglossus.

- Its nucleus lies just beneath the floor of the fourth ventricle. Different groups of cells supply different muscles: intrinsic tongue muscles, then genioglossus, hyoglossus and styloglossus, from rostral to caudal.
- It leaves the medulla in the groove between the pyramid and the olive as 10 to 15 rootlets, which gather into two bundles, pierce the dura and pass through the **hypoglossal canal** (in the occipital bone, anterosuperior to the occipital condyle).
- In the neck it makes a half-spiral turn round the inferior vagal ganglion, descends between the internal jugular vein and internal carotid artery, and passes forward above the greater cornu of the hyoid to reach the tongue.

**Examination:** look inside the mouth at the size of the tongue, symmetry, wasting, fasciculation (small twitches) on protrusion, and deviation to one side.

## The jugular foramen

The glossopharyngeal, vagus and accessory nerves all leave the skull through the jugular foramen. A tumour or thrombosis here can therefore damage all three together, and the result is **jugular foramen syndrome**: weakness of the muscles of the pharynx and larynx with multiple cranial nerve palsies. Causes include a jugular paraganglioma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma and jugular bulb thrombosis, for example after otitis media.

