## Examining the cranial nerves

Systematic examination of the cranial nerves is part of the examination of every patient with a neurological problem. It may show a lesion of a nerve nucleus, its central connections, or the lower motor neurons. Examine the nerves in numerical order, but group III, IV and VI together because their function is similar. In a hurry, the bare minimum is to check the fundi, visual fields, pupil size and reaction, eye movements and facial movements.

## Cranial nerves in head injury

The cranial nerves most often injured in traumatic brain injury are the olfactory, optic, oculomotor and trochlear nerves, the first and second divisions of the trigeminal nerve, and the facial and auditory nerves. In basal skull fractures the olfactory, facial and auditory nerves are the most liable to injury, though any nerve can be damaged. Bruising of the periorbital tissues (the "raccoon" or "panda bear" appearance) points to a fracture of the anterior skull base.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The olfactory pathway runs directly to the olfactory cortex and bypasses the thalamus. The optic nerve ends in the thalamus, and the other cranial nerves are attached to the brainstem or cervical cord." %}
Which sensory cranial nerve projects directly to the cerebral cortex rather than through the thalamus?
- A. Optic nerve
- B. Vestibulocochlear nerve
- C. Olfactory nerve
- D. Glossopharyngeal nerve
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="In conductive hearing loss, bone conduction is louder than air conduction (Rinne negative) and the Weber test lateralises to the affected ear. In sensorineural loss, air conduction is still louder and the Weber lateralises to the normal ear." %}
A patient with unilateral hearing loss has a negative Rinne test in the left ear (bone conduction louder than air conduction) and the Weber test lateralises to the left. This most likely indicates:
- A. Left conductive hearing loss
- B. Left sensorineural hearing loss
- C. Right conductive hearing loss
- D. Right sensorineural hearing loss
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The spinal root of the accessory nerve supplies trapezius after crossing the posterior triangle, where it is superficial. Paralysis of trapezius causes wasting and a drooping shoulder. Tongue wasting is from the hypoglossal nerve, and facial sensation is from the trigeminal nerve." %}
The accessory nerve is cut in the posterior triangle of the neck. Which finding is expected?
- A. Wasting of one side of the tongue
- B. Loss of sensation over the cheek
- C. Inability to close the eye tightly
- D. Drooping of the shoulder with wasting of trapezius
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The labyrinthine segment is the shortest and narrowest part of the facial nerve, in the narrowest part of the bony canal, and it lacks anastomosing arterial cascades. Oedema or inflammation can therefore compress it easily." %}
Which segment of the facial nerve is most easily compressed by oedema or inflammation?
- A. Tympanic (horizontal) segment
- B. Labyrinthine segment
- C. Mastoid (vertical) segment
- D. Extracranial segment
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The gag reflex uses the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) as its afferent limb and the vagus nerve (CN X) as its efferent limb." %}
Which pair of nerves carries the afferent and efferent limbs of the gag reflex?
- A. V and VII
- B. VII and IX
- C. IX and X
- D. X and XI
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The glossopharyngeal, vagus and accessory nerves all leave the skull through the jugular foramen. A tumour there can therefore cause multiple cranial nerve palsies, called jugular foramen syndrome." %}
A tumour at the jugular foramen would most likely damage which group of cranial nerves?
- A. IX, X and XI
- B. X, XI and XII
- C. VII, VIII and IX
- D. V, VI and VII
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="How many cranial nerves are there, and how are they classed?" %}
There are 12 pairs, numbered I to XII from front to back. Some are purely sensory, some purely motor and the rest mixed. Some also carry parasympathetic fibres.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which cranial nerve attaches to the spinal cord, and which passes straight to the cortex?" %}
The accessory nerve (spinal root) attaches to the upper cervical spinal cord. The olfactory nerve is the only sensory cranial nerve that projects directly to the cerebral cortex without relaying in the thalamus.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Give the openings of the skull used by the three divisions of the trigeminal nerve." %}
The ophthalmic division uses the superior orbital fissure, the maxillary division the foramen rotundum, and the mandibular division the foramen ovale.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How do you test the corneal reflex, and which nerves are involved?" %}
Touch the cornea and watch for eye closure. The afferent limb is the trigeminal nerve (CN V) and the efferent limb is the facial nerve (CN VII).
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How do the Rinne and Weber tests differ in conductive and sensorineural deafness?" %}
In conductive loss, bone conduction is louder than air conduction (Rinne negative) and the Weber lateralises to the affected ear. In sensorineural loss, air conduction is still louder than bone conduction and the Weber lateralises to the normal ear.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What happens when the spinal accessory nerve is injured in the posterior triangle, and how is it tested?" %}
Trapezius is paralysed and wastes, the shoulder drops, and raising the arm above the head is difficult. It is tested by asking the patient to shrug the shoulders (trapezius) and to turn the head against resistance (opposite sternocleidomastoid).
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe trigeminal neuralgia and its first-line treatment." %}
It is a one-sided, electric-shock facial pain, usually in the V2 or V3 area, lasting seconds to minutes and set off by a trigger zone. Causes include compression by the superior cerebellar artery, a cerebellopontine angle tumour and multiple sclerosis. Carbamazepine is the drug of choice, and microvascular decompression is the usual operation if drugs fail.
{% /viva %}

